  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper text is present. Some table layout is flattened, but the reported model-to-value assignments are identifiable.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports harmful-completion rates for multi-turn jailbreak attacks against safety-aligned models. This is a different intervention from the token-prefill, suffix, and decoding methods in V4, but it tests whether adversarial prompting can elicit harmful responses. It does not test fine-tuning, token-position KL, parameter localization, or why safety is shallow.",
    "gate_note": "The study evaluates multi-turn adversarial conversations and reports Bin-ASR and StrongReject scores. It does not test fine-tuning, compliant-prefix prefilling, adversarial suffixes, decoding changes, or the first-token mechanism. Rows record the PLAGUE results and its reported GOAT-based component ablation; comparator attacks and additional ablations are not included.",
    "test_reason": "The study builds and evaluates a black-box multi-turn jailbreak framework (PLAGUE) that escalates context over up to six conversational turns. It does not prefill a compliant response start, optimise suffixes or change decoding, so it does not test V4's mechanism. It also does not fine-tune models (V1/V2), measure per-token KL (V3), localise parameters (V5), explain shallowness (V6) or train deeper alignment (V7). Its success rates are a related but different construct: conversational jailbreak success, not bypass by getting past the opening tokens. Qi et al. (2310.03693) is cited only for an evaluator temperature setting. Table 3 is the authors' own component ablation of their method.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that PLAGUE, a modular multi-turn framework (planner, primer, finisher, reflection, strategy retrieval), reaches state-of-the-art ASR, up to 97.8% SRE. They add that component contributions differ by target model. Claude Opus 4.1's best result (67.3% SRE) needs a Crescendo finisher instead of GOAT. They make no claim about token-depth of safety.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Black-box multi-turn adversarial conversations with planning, context-building, reflection, and retrieval; no fine-tuning, token-level KL measurement, compliant-prefix intervention, suffix optimization, or decoding change.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Attack success varies by target model and attack configuration. The paper reports component ablations and different results for GOAT and Crescendo as finisher modules.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Agentic multi-turn context-escalation attacks elicit harmful HarmBench completions from frontier models within six turns. Bin-ASR/SRE reach 0.662/0.814 on o3, up to 0.945/0.978 on Deepseek-R1, and 0.467/0.673 on Claude Opus 4.1 with a Crescendo finisher (Table 4).",
        "proposed_claim": "Safety-aligned models' refusals can be defeated by gradual multi-turn conversational escalation, without access to weights or response prefixes.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
